AUDIO: 'A lot of you pay close to $500 a year just for this tool. But there is one free Burp alternative that almost nobody in Western security even talks about. And it\'s not just a proxy. It\'s a full offensive security platform with its own'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scripting language. A visual fuzzer, passive scanning, reverse connections, and a workflow that looks like Burp got rebuilt for automation. This is Yakit. And the engine underneath all of it is'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Yak Lang. It\'s powerful. It\'s honestly impressive and it\'s also free. But at the same time, it\'s also heavy Chinese first and not the cleanest tool to learn on day one. So the question is not is'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this better than Burp, but rather who should actually use this? [music] All right. Now, first up to the plate here, why should you care if you\'re not'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a full-time pen tester? Right, we have Burp, Yakit, Kaddio, Zap. These tools all start from the same core idea. They sit between your browser and the internet. That means every request your'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browser sends can go through the security pipeline before it reaches the server. Burr became the standard because it made that workflow practical. It does a really good job. Intercept, inspect,'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'send to the repeater, send to intruder, scan document, repeat. That\'s the process. But Burp\'s free version is intentionally limited. There\'s no full scanner. Intruder is throttled. All the goodies live behind the pro license.'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Yakit\'s thing is basically what if that whole workflow was free, scriptable, and built in to one platform. If you enjoy coding tools to speed up your workflow, be sure to subscribe. We have videos'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coming out all the time. All right. Now, let me show you how Yakit kind of works here. Say I have a front end talking to an API and the bug report says saving the profile fails. Sometimes the browser'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'console just gives me a useless 400. So instead of guessing, I open Yakin\'s MITM proxy, point my browser at it, and install the certificate so I can read'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'HTTPS traffic. Now I click save in the app, and Yakit shows me the exact API request. So here is the method, here\'s the URL, I got my headers, then we got'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the off token, and I got the JSON body the front end was actually sent. So essentially, I\'m no longer debugging the request I thought my app sent. I\'m'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'debugging the real one. Now, maybe user ID is being sent as a string instead of a number. Or maybe the front end is sending an empty array when the backend expects the field to be omitted. Now I'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pause the request before it even reaches the server. Change one value and forwarding. If the server accepts the edited request, I probably have a front-end bug. If it still fails, I\'m'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looking at backend validation off or schema handling. Then I can send the same request into Yakit\'s web fuzzer. In the replay, it just replays it with the variations. I can remove a fields. I can'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'change a rule. I can send a malformed date, try a huge string, you name it. That same loop here is really capture, inspect, edit, replay, all of that varies is the whole reason these'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
